# Your First Selecto Query: Domains, Parameters, and Automatic Joins

Start here before the long guide. In about ten minutes you will compile a
query, see exactly when a join is needed, and keep external values out of SQL.
No database, Ecto Repo, migrations, or seed data are needed. Assertions make
the expected behavior executable, rather than relying on printed SQL alone.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_core!()
```

## 1) Describe the data once

The application owns the tables, keys, and allowed relationship paths. An
association describes how to reach a table; declaring it does **not** mean
every query joins that table. This small domain deliberately has two joins.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions

domain = %{
  name: "First orders",
  source: %{
    source_table: "orders",
    primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :status, :customer_id, :warehouse_id],
    redact_fields: [],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer},
      status: %{type: :string},
      customer_id: %{type: :integer},
      warehouse_id: %{type: :integer}
    },
    associations: %{
      customer: %{queryable: :customers, field: :customer, owner_key: :customer_id, related_key: :id},
      warehouse: %{queryable: :warehouses, field: :warehouse, owner_key: :warehouse_id, related_key: :id}
    }
  },
  schemas: Map.new([:customers, :warehouses], fn table ->
    {table, %{
      source_table: Atom.to_string(table),
      primary_key: :id,
      fields: [:id, :name],
      redact_fields: [],
      columns: %{id: %{type: :integer}, name: %{type: :string}},
      associations: %{}
    }}
  end),
  joins: %{customer: %{type: :left}, warehouse: %{type: :left}}
}

runtime = Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only)
base = Selecto.configure(domain, runtime)
```

The explicit runtime chooses the SQL dialect and adapter. `:compile_only` is
an opaque placeholder for this example, **not** a connection you can execute
against. A live application supplies its adapter-owned connection handle.

## 2) Compose, then compile

Query construction is immutable. Reuse `base` for independent branches.
`to_sql/1` compiles but does not run the query; filters carry bound values.

```elixir
open_orders =
  base
  |> Selecto.select(["id", "status"])
  |> Selecto.filter({"status", "open"})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"id", :asc})
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

{sql, params} = Selecto.to_sql(open_orders)
assert params == ["open"]
assert sql =~ "$1"
refute sql =~ "'open'"
refute sql =~ ~r/\bjoin\b/i
assert base.set.filtered == []
%{sql: sql, params: params}
```

## 3) Requesting related data selects the join

There is no separate join list to keep in sync with the projection. Selecting
`customer.name` activates the customer path, but not the warehouse path.

```elixir
with_customer = Selecto.select(open_orders, ["customer.name"])
{customer_sql, customer_params} = Selecto.to_sql(with_customer)
assert customer_sql =~ ~r/\bjoin\s+customers\b/i
refute customer_sql =~ ~r/\bjoin\s+warehouses\b/i
assert customer_params == ["open"]
{unchanged_sql, _} = Selecto.to_sql(open_orders)
refute unchanged_sql =~ ~r/\bjoin\b/i
%{sql: customer_sql, params: customer_params}
```

## 4) A join can be needed without selecting its columns

Related fields in filters and ordering also need their joins. In a real
query, removing a visible column is not enough to remove a join if another
clause still references that relationship.

```elixir
filter_only =
  base
  |> Selecto.select(["id"])
  |> Selecto.filter({"customer.name", "Ada"})

order_only =
  base
  |> Selecto.select(["id"])
  |> Selecto.order_by([{"customer.name", :asc}, {"id", :asc}])

for query <- [filter_only, order_only] do
  {clause_sql, _params} = Selecto.to_sql(query)
  assert clause_sql =~ ~r/\bjoin\s+customers\b/i
  refute clause_sql =~ ~r/\bjoin\s+warehouses\b/i
end
```

A left join followed by a non-null customer predicate can exclude unmatched
orders. Also, selecting a to-many relationship can multiply root rows. Use
[selection shapes/subselects](selecto_selection_shapes_subselects_retargets.livemd)
when you need one order with a collection of line items, rather than a flat
order-item row for every match.

## 5) Values and identifiers are different boundaries

This deliberately hostile **value** must remain a parameter. Parameterization
does not decide whether a value is meaningful or authorized for your app.
Keep user-selected fields/orderings behind an application allowlist too.

```elixir
external_status = "open' OR 1=1 --"
{safe_sql, safe_params} =
  base
  |> Selecto.select(["id"])
  |> Selecto.filter({"status", external_status})
  |> Selecto.to_sql()

assert safe_params == [external_status]
refute safe_sql =~ external_status

allowed_sorts = %{"oldest" => {"id", :asc}, "newest" => {"id", :desc}}
assert {:ok, {"id", :desc}} = Map.fetch(allowed_sorts, "newest")
assert :error = Map.fetch(allowed_sorts, "id; DROP TABLE orders")
```

No arbitrary field name becomes an atom or SQL fragment. For a sealed query
boundary, continue with [strict mode](selecto_strict_mode_workbook.livemd).
For a real database-backed application flow, next try
[pagination](selecto_pagination_workbook.livemd) and
[tenant-scoped reads](selecto_tenant_reads_workbook.livemd).
